body:
## The idea, in a paragraph

Let the Hybrid compiler parallelize a restricted image filter and emit a small, independently checkable description of which pixels each worker may read and write. For example, a horizontal blur followed by a vertical blur and a diagonal sharpen needs pixels beyond each tile boundary. If each worker clamps its reads at its own tile edge, the code can be race-free and still draw visible seams. I propose checking two properties together: each output pixel has exactly one writer, and every stage receives the same neighboring pixels it would have received in the serial program. The second property is what makes this a language experiment.

## Which assumption it breaks

[Language rule C1](https://github.com/limitlessprojects/Go-Hybrid/blob/12a86bf96808ff6398e725cf73205bb0d200b904/SPEC-LANGUAGE.md#L1193-L1205) puts a task boundary at a call written by the programmer. Try letting the compiler introduce the tasks for one narrow class of pure loops. Restrict the first experiment to rectangular integer domains, fixed offsets, read-only input and a specified clamp-to-frame rule. Describe an output tile by half-open coordinates. Work backwards through the stages to infer each intermediate rectangle. Translate the rectangle once per sample offset, clamp its two extreme corners to the original frame, then take their bounding rectangle. Clamping before combining is important. A sample entirely past the right edge still reads the last column.

## Why this is a breakthrough, not an increment

A compiler-introduced task could carry a checkable reason that its scheduling change preserves the value of every pixel. An optimizer can suggest a bad plan and a small independent checker can reject it. The checker matches the final rectangles against the canonical partition exactly once, then verifies containment of each translated and clamped read in the preceding stage. Pointwise equivalence follows by induction over the stages within this limited language.

Bounds inference and tiling are established ideas. [Halide's scheduling tutorial](https://github.com/halide/Halide/blob/main/tutorial/lesson_08_scheduling_2.cpp) is the relevant baseline. The question for Hybrid is whether to admit compiler-created tasks on the strength of a checked plan without extending its general-purpose alias analysis. In the prototype, workers share read-only source pixels, allocate their own intermediates and write independent spans into one destination. A Hybrid implementation would have to enforce those permissions, including excluding external mutable aliases and preventing a destination slice from growing into its neighbor. The Go experiment alone establishes neither condition for Hybrid programs.

## How we would know it works (or doesn't)

Codex implemented and ran this standalone prototype on macOS arm64 with Go 1.26.9. I authorized publication of its source and results. It compares four schedules against an independent serial reference on 120 seeded cases. These cover one-pixel axes, irregular last tiles, signed filter weights, and sample offsets larger than the frame.

- All 480 comparisons matched byte for byte. A deliberately wrong implementation that clamps reads to the tile differed on 432 of 627 pixels in a 33 × 19 fixture.
- The plan checker rejected a missing output tile, a duplicate writer and a missing input border. The small-case run completed under the Go race detector.
- On a 1920 × 1080 frame with 96-pixel tiles, the four-worker upper bound for simultaneously needed intermediate pixel buffers was 78,008 bytes versus 4,147,200 bytes for two full intermediates. These are calculated pixel storage bounds. They exclude the shared source, final output, allocation overhead and memory retained by the Go runtime.
- Final medians over seven interleaved runs were 46.44 ms for serial whole-frame, 54.18 ms for serial tiled, 17.31 ms for four-worker whole-frame, and 21.57 ms for four-worker tiled. In an earlier run before adding the independent plan verifier, the four-worker comparison went the other way (18.80 vs 17.99 ms). The consistent result is the smaller intermediate footprint. These runs do not establish a speed advantage over a parallel whole-frame implementation.

The next milestone is to lower **one actual Hybrid loop** to Go, validate the plan before emission, and compare emitted results to serial execution. Intentionally shrink a halo, duplicate a tile, and add a data-dependent index. Reject the bad plans or fall back to serial. Abandon this as a language feature if a hand-written Go library can enforce the same restrictions at the boundary as simply, or if maintaining the checker requires implementing the optimizer a second time.

## Cost honesty

Sparse footprints can make bounding rectangles wasteful. Several wide stages can turn each small tile into nearly a full-frame job. The compiler needs a serial fallback. The prototype models byte images, integer taps and clamp-to-frame edges. It does not handle rescaling, wraparound, arbitrary Go calls, runtime-dependent sample positions or observable side effects. Runtime timings cover the four schedulers. They exclude the separate plan-verification pass.


<details>
<summary>Reproduce with the Go standard library (source, run commands and raw results)</summary>

Copy the following file as `tile.go`, then run:

```sh
go run tile.go
go run -race tile.go -check-only
```

```go
// Run with Go 1.26: go run tile.go. Use -check-only for the small oracle cases.
package main

import (
	"bytes"
	"crypto/sha256"
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"image"
	"math/rand"
	"runtime"
	"sort"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type tap struct{ x, y, weight int }
type stage struct {
	taps          []tap
	bias, divisor int
}

type plane struct {
	buf []byte
	box image.Rectangle
}

func alloc(box image.Rectangle) plane {
	return plane{make([]byte, box.Dx()*box.Dy()), box}
}
func coord(x, limit int) int { return max(0, min(x, limit-1)) }

func input(w, h int) plane {
	out := alloc(image.Rect(0, 0, w, h))
	rng := rand.New(rand.NewSource(118))
	for i := range out.buf {
		out.buf[i] = byte(rng.Intn(256))
	}
	return out
}

// The clamp applies at every stage boundary, before taking the bounding box.
// This matters for a tile whose entire translated footprint misses the frame.
func needed(box image.Rectangle, taps []tap, w, h int) image.Rectangle {
	var bounds image.Rectangle
	for _, t := range taps {
		x0, x1 := coord(box.Min.X+t.x, w), coord(box.Max.X-1+t.x, w)
		y0, y1 := coord(box.Min.Y+t.y, h), coord(box.Max.Y-1+t.y, h)
		bounds = bounds.Union(image.Rect(x0, y0, x1+1, y1+1))
	}
	return bounds
}

// All schedulers use this identical kernel. Bounds of dst need not equal box.
func render(src, dst plane, box image.Rectangle, s stage, w, h int) {
	stride, outStride := src.box.Dx(), dst.box.Dx()
	for y := box.Min.Y; y < box.Max.Y; y++ {
		k := (y-dst.box.Min.Y)*outStride + box.Min.X - dst.box.Min.X
		for x := box.Min.X; x < box.Max.X; x++ {
			acc := s.bias
			for _, t := range s.taps {
				px, py := coord(x+t.x, w), coord(y+t.y, h)
				i := (py-src.box.Min.Y)*stride + px - src.box.Min.X
				acc += t.weight * int(src.buf[i])
			}
			dst.buf[k] = byte(max(0, min(255, acc/s.divisor)))
			k++
		}
	}
}

func rectangles(w, h, side int) []image.Rectangle {
	var boxes []image.Rectangle
	for y := 0; y < h; y += side {
		for x := 0; x < w; x += side {
			boxes = append(boxes, image.Rect(x, y, min(x+side, w), min(y+side, h)))
		}
	}
	return boxes
}

func forEach(boxes []image.Rectangle, workers int, job func(image.Rectangle)) {
	if workers == 1 {
		for _, b := range boxes {
			job(b)
		}
		return
	}
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	ch := make(chan image.Rectangle, workers)
	for range workers {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			for b := range ch {
				job(b)
			}
		}()
	}
	for _, b := range boxes {
		ch <- b
	}
	close(ch)
	wg.Wait()
}

func full(src plane, ops []stage, side, workers int) plane {
	frame := src.box
	boxes := rectangles(frame.Dx(), frame.Dy(), side)
	for _, s := range ops {
		next := alloc(frame)
		forEach(boxes, workers, func(b image.Rectangle) { render(src, next, b, s, frame.Dx(), frame.Dy()) })
		src = next
	}
	return src
}

func plan(out image.Rectangle, ops []stage, w, h int) []image.Rectangle {
	boxes := make([]image.Rectangle, len(ops))
	boxes[len(boxes)-1] = out
	for i := len(ops) - 1; i > 0; i-- {
		boxes[i-1] = needed(boxes[i], ops[i].taps, w, h)
	}
	return boxes
}

func tiled(src plane, ops []stage, side, workers int) plane {
	w, h := src.box.Dx(), src.box.Dy()
	dst := alloc(src.box)
	forEach(rectangles(w, h, side), workers, func(b image.Rectangle) {
		prev := src
		for i, box := range plan(b, ops, w, h) {
			next := dst
			if i < len(ops)-1 {
				next = alloc(box)
			}
			render(prev, next, box, ops[i], w, h)
			prev = next
		}
	})
	return dst
}

// Independent full-frame reference. The negative control deliberately clips
// reads to the output tile. The result then has seams even without data races.
func reference(src plane, ops []stage, tile int) plane {
	w, h := src.box.Dx(), src.box.Dy()
	for _, s := range ops {
		next := alloc(src.box)
		for i := range next.buf {
			x, y := i%w, i/w
			loX, loY := (x/tile)*tile, (y/tile)*tile
			hiX, hiY := min(w, loX+tile)-1, min(h, loY+tile)-1
			sum := s.bias
			for _, t := range s.taps {
				px, py := max(loX, min(x+t.x, hiX)), max(loY, min(y+t.y, hiY))
				sum += int(src.buf[py*w+px]) * t.weight
			}
			q := sum / s.divisor
			if q < 0 {
				q = 0
			} else if q > 255 {
				q = 255
			}
			next.buf[i] = byte(q)
		}
		src = next
	}
	return src
}

var filters = []stage{
	{[]tap{{-2, 0, 1}, {0, 0, 2}, {1, 0, 1}}, 0, 4},
	{[]tap{{0, -1, 2}, {0, 0, 1}, {0, 2, 1}}, 0, 4},
	{[]tap{{-1, -1, -1}, {0, 0, 3}, {1, 1, -1}}, 0, 1},
}

// Verify an emitted schedule independently of needed(). A constant translation
// followed by a per-axis clamp is monotone, so the two extreme corners of
// each translated rectangle bound every read. Output rectangles are checked
// against a canonical partition, which gives each destination one writer.
func certify(plans [][]image.Rectangle, frame image.Rectangle, ops []stage, side int) bool {
	expected := make(map[image.Rectangle]bool)
	for _, box := range rectangles(frame.Dx(), frame.Dy(), side) {
		expected[box] = true
	}
	clamped := func(p image.Point) image.Point {
		return image.Pt(coord(p.X, frame.Dx()), coord(p.Y, frame.Dy()))
	}
	for _, boxes := range plans {
		if len(boxes) != len(ops) || len(boxes) == 0 {
			return false
		}
		tile := boxes[len(boxes)-1]
		if !expected[tile] {
			return false
		}
		delete(expected, tile)
		producer := frame
		for i, box := range boxes {
			if box.Empty() || !box.In(frame) {
				return false
			}
			for _, t := range ops[i].taps {
				offset := image.Pt(t.x, t.y)
				first := clamped(box.Min.Add(offset))
				last := clamped(box.Max.Sub(image.Pt(1, 1)).Add(offset))
				if !first.In(producer) || !last.In(producer) {
					return false
				}
			}
			producer = box
		}
	}
	return len(expected) == 0
}

func checkCertificate() {
	frame := image.Rect(0, 0, 33, 19)
	var plans [][]image.Rectangle
	for _, b := range rectangles(33, 19, 8) {
		plans = append(plans, plan(b, filters, 33, 19))
	}
	if !certify(plans, frame, filters, 8) {
		panic("valid certificate rejected")
	}
	if certify(plans[1:], frame, filters, 8) {
		panic("missing tile accepted")
	}
	dup := append([][]image.Rectangle{plans[0]}, plans...)
	if certify(dup, frame, filters, 8) {
		panic("duplicate tile accepted")
	}
	local := make([][]image.Rectangle, len(plans))
	for i, row := range plans {
		last := row[len(row)-1]
		local[i] = []image.Rectangle{last, last, last}
	}
	if certify(local, frame, filters, 8) {
		panic("missing halo accepted")
	}
	fmt.Println("independent plan verifier: valid plan accepted; missing tile, duplicate tile and missing halo rejected")
}

func check() {
	checkCertificate()
	rng := rand.New(rand.NewSource(586))
	cases := 0
	for trial := 0; trial < 120; trial++ {
		w, h, side := 1+rng.Intn(22), 1+rng.Intn(17), 1+rng.Intn(11)
		if trial%6 == 0 {
			w = 1
		}
		if trial%7 == 0 {
			h = 1
		}
		ops := make([]stage, 1+rng.Intn(5))
		for i := range ops {
			s := stage{bias: rng.Intn(160), divisor: 1 + rng.Intn(5)}
			for range 1 + rng.Intn(4) {
				s.taps = append(s.taps, tap{rng.Intn(15) - 7, rng.Intn(15) - 7, rng.Intn(6) - 2})
			}
			ops[i] = s
		}
		src := input(w, h)
		want := reference(src, ops, max(w, h))
		for _, v := range []plane{full(src, ops, side, 1), full(src, ops, side, 4), tiled(src, ops, side, 1), tiled(src, ops, side, 4)} {
			if !bytes.Equal(want.buf, v.buf) {
				panic(fmt.Sprintf("oracle mismatch trial %d", trial))
			}
			cases++
		}
	}
	src := input(33, 19)
	good, bad := reference(src, filters, 33), reference(src, filters, 8)
	diff := 0
	for i := range good.buf {
		if good.buf[i] != bad.buf[i] {
			diff++
		}
	}
	if diff == 0 {
		panic("negative control failed")
	}
	fmt.Printf("oracle comparisons=%d/480; negative control mismatches=%d/%d pixels\n", cases, diff, len(good.buf))
}

func scratch(w, h, side int) int {
	maxPair := 0
	for _, b := range rectangles(w, h, side) {
		prev := 0
		for _, p := range plan(b, filters, w, h)[:len(filters)-1] {
			n := p.Dx() * p.Dy()
			maxPair = max(maxPair, prev+n)
			prev = n
		}
	}
	return maxPair
}

func main() {
	only := flag.Bool("check-only", false, "run oracle and negative control only")
	flag.Parse()
	check()
	if *only {
		return
	}
	w, h, side, workers := 1920, 1080, 96, 4
	runtime.GOMAXPROCS(workers)
	src := input(w, h)
	names := []string{"full-serial", "tiled-serial", "tiled-four", "full-four"}
	runs := []func() plane{
		func() plane { return full(src, filters, side, 1) },
		func() plane { return tiled(src, filters, side, 1) },
		func() plane { return tiled(src, filters, side, workers) },
		func() plane { return full(src, filters, side, workers) },
	}
	durations := make([][]time.Duration, len(runs))
	want := sha256.Sum256(runs[0]().buf)
	for round := -1; round < 7; round++ {
		for shift := range runs {
			i := (round + 1 + shift) % len(runs)
			start := time.Now()
			s := runs[i]()
			d := time.Since(start)
			if sha256.Sum256(s.buf) != want {
				panic("benchmark mismatch")
			}
			if round >= 0 {
				durations[i] = append(durations[i], d)
			}
		}
	}
	fmt.Printf("%s %s/%s; frame=%dx%d tile=%d workers=%d sha256=%x\n", runtime.Version(), runtime.GOOS, runtime.GOARCH, w, h, side, workers, want)
	for i, ds := range durations {
		fmt.Printf("%s raw(ms):", names[i])
		for _, d := range ds {
			fmt.Printf(" %.2f", float64(d)/1e6)
		}
		sort.Slice(ds, func(i, j int) bool { return ds[i] < ds[j] })
		fmt.Printf(" median=%.2f\n", float64(ds[len(ds)/2])/1e6)
	}
	fmt.Printf("full-frame intermediate pixel bytes=%d; four-worker tile upper bound=%d\n", 2*w*h, workers*scratch(w, h, side))
}
